---
title: Clean Water for Every Community
order: 3
icon: droplet
---

Every day, 500,000 Nairobians in Kibera, Mathare, Mukuru, and Korogocho pay cartels five times the official tariff for water that carries typhoid and cholera. Nairobi's pipes leak 40 percent of our water before it reaches a single tap. This is not a resource problem — it is a leadership failure.

We will drill and rehabilitate boreholes in every ward where piped water is unreliable. Each borehole will get a solar-powered pump, a storage tank, and a community-managed kiosk selling water at a regulated price — capped at 5 shillings per 20-litre jerrycan. Every informal settlement must have at least one public water point per 200 households, built to Kenya Bureau of Standards specifications and run by an elected water committee.

We will overhaul the Nairobi City Water and Sewerage Company with one mandate: cut non-revenue water from 40 percent to below 20 percent within three years. Pipe replacement, pressure management, and modern metering will make it happen. For communities beyond the piped network, we will tender water trucking contracts transparently — every truck tracked by GPS, every delivery verified, every shilling accounted for. Rooftop rainwater harvesting systems will be installed in every public school and health centre across the county.

The era of opaque water contracts ends now. Every water project — its budget, contractor, timeline, and status — will live on a public Water Accountability Portal. Communities will report broken boreholes, leaking pipes, and price-gouging vendors through a direct hotline. Clean water is not a privilege for those who can afford private connections. It is a fundamental right, and this county government will guarantee it — or step aside.
